116
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
So let's get started. shall we

117
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Write me a letter? send it by name

118
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Email, that is, folks.

119
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

120
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
So this question comes from Dave

121
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
And Dave says in some parts of the country

122
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,000
there's still snow on the ground

123
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
and plastic Santa Claus is on the roof.

124
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Yet here I sit thinking about summer camp,

125
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Scouts are joining our troop

126
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
and here we are asking for all kinds of things.

127
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:36,000
We welcome them. then we ask for troop registration funds,

128
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,000
We ask them to make a commitment to a week of summer camp

129
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and an extra $100 payment. all in the same breath.

130
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
How do we sell the virtues of sending your son off to summer camp

131
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
with people you just met?

132
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
And how do we convince our incoming Scouts and their families

133
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
that summer camp is something that they need to do

134
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
and that they want to do?

135
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
How about that for an upcoming topic?

136
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Well, Dave, thanks,

137
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
And here it is.

138
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
We talk a lot about summer camp,

139
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
You know. hopefully we can derive from some of the things

140
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
that we've written and said in the past

141
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
some ideas to promote around.

142
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
But you're right, you know,

143
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Here we are.

144
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,000
It's still wintertime,

145
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Summer is a long way away

146
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
And while a lot of families are beginning to think

147
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
about making plans, when we go to them and we say,

148
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
hey, it's time to sign up for summer camp-

149
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And, by the way, you know you got to cut us a check-

150
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
It may be a little tough to get them to imagine

151
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
exactly what it's going to be like:

152
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Boys who have just crossed over and joined the troop

153
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and families who, you know are still kind of waiting

154
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
to see how this is going to pan out.

155
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
How do we sell them on signing up now

156
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
and getting to summer camp?

157
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Here's what I would say to them- and you know

158
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
it's not necessarily just a sales pitch for this time of year,

159
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,000
It's for any time of year.

160
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
If folks aren't sure whether or not summer camp

161
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
is a good idea for their 10 and a half or 11-year-old boy

162
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
after he's completed the fifth grade,

163
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
And if they're not too sure whether or not

164
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
they want to send them off to summer camp for the week,

165
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
what I do is I tell them this.

166
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Often note that their boys returned from the first week

167
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
of summer camp having grown up a little bit

168
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
and they're a lot more appreciative of the comforts of home.

169
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
So there's a couple of very practical things that happen

170
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
when a boy attends a week of summer camp.

171
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
What you have to imagine is this

172
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Camp is a challenging and exciting experience.

173
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
There's a lot of adventure to it.

174
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
There's a lot of new things that are going to happen.

175
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
But if you think about this,

176
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
think about what the average scout does

177
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
during a week of summer camp.

178
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Think about what this is going to do for your son.

179
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
He's going to wake up every morning

180
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
and he's going to wake up on schedule.

181
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
And he's not going to wake up

182
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
because he's heard an alarm clock, necessarily,

183
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
or some adult is coming by and making sure that he's awake.

184
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
He's going to wake up on schedule

185
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
because the boys in his troop are going to make sure

186
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,000
that he is being supported and he gets up

187
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
and he's ready to go

188
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
And pretty soon he's going to be doing that all on his own.

189
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
He's going to keep his personal space and his things

190
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
reasonably orderly and clean,

191
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
without a whole lot of intervention on the part of adults,

192
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
without a whole lot of nagging or carrying on.

193
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
like you know, more than likely is happening at home.

194
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
If he goes to summer camp with our troop,

195
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
where we have a dining hall program-

196
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
and I would imagine that's pretty much the common arrangement

197
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
in most summer camps that you will be attending-

198
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
He's going to actually eat three meals a day,

199
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
sitting at a table with seven other people.

200
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
His food is going to be served family style.

201
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
He's going to be shared amongst the whole table.

202
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Each meal is going to begin by reciting grace

203
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
and it's going to end when they're dismissed

204
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And scouts are going to take time during the week

205
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
serving as waiters for their table.

206
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
They're going to set up and serve

207
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and clean up after meals.

208
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
You know that doesn't happen in very many homes anymore.

209
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
Not three meals a day, seven days a week.

210
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
A lot of families do make time and they have dinners

211
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and they have breakfast, maybe together,

212
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
But not three meals a day, seven days a week.

213
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
I don't think anybody manages to pull that one off

214
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
If you do more power to you.

215
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:29,000
But if you just think about that aspect of camp

216
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
just around, that very simple thing

217
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
of going and sharing meals with their fellow scouts

218
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
and having some responsibility for it,

219
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
That's a pretty big deal.

220
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
That's a pretty big deal.

221
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
He's going to need to manage his money

222
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
without a lot of oversight.

223
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
That means he's got a plan and budget

224
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
for his visits to the training post.

225
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,000
He's got to think his way through

226
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
about how he's going to spend his money

227
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
and that's probably the first time in his life

228
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
that he's had, you know, a pocketful of money

229
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
that he's going to need to manage in. that was

230
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
without his mom and dad being right there.

231
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,000
So that's another pretty interesting thing

232
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
that you might not really think about

233
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
when you think about a week of summer camp.

234
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
He's going to follow his own schedule of activities

235
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
and he's going to set that schedule

236
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
according to his own interests

237
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and he's going to have a fairly broad menu

238
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
of things that he gets to choose from.

239
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Now a lot of camps have a first year program.

240
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
that's a pretty set schedule

241
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and usually some time in there

242
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
for self-determined activities

243
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
and maybe a couple of merit badges

244
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
and things like that.

245
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
That's a pretty big deal.

246
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
That's going to be a pretty big deal.

247
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
He's going to be responsible to find his way

248
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
to and from those activities

249
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,000
and to plan his time

250
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
and to make sure he gets to them on time

251
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
and things like that. you know,

252
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
this is something that he may have experienced

253
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
somewhat in school up to this point

254
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
but that he will experience later on,

255
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
especially in the coming years

256
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
in high school, and high school you know,

257
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
that kind of self-awareness

258
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
and responsibility of planning things

259
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
and he's going to get a pretty solid week of that.

260
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,000
These are some of the things

261
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
that we don't often think about

262
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
that are very important

263
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
to that summer camp experience.

264
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,000
He's going to be working together

265
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
with his troop and patrol.

266
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
He's going to be responsible for himself.

267
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
He's going to need to take care of his things.

268
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
He's going to have a set schedule of activities.

269
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
It's a pretty big deal

270
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
If you're running your troop by the patrol system,

271
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
if your youth leaders have a lot of latitude

272
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
with what they do and how they run things.

273
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
any scout who's going to camp for his first year

274
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
is going to learn a lot more than he does

275
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
just going and attending merit badge sessions.

276
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
I mean that's great.

277
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:46,000
It's great that they get to go

278
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
and attend these merit badge sessions.

279
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
They get to learn skills and interesting things

280
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and they walk away with merit badge or two.

281
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
or if they go to that first year program,

282
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
they're working on scout skills,

283
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:57,000
usually up to first class,

284
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and they spend a lot of time doing that.

285
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
that's okay.

286
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
That's a great thing for him to do

287
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
and a lot of times that's what we concentrate on

288
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
as the direct benefits of going to summer camp.

289
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
but I want to think of all those intangible things.

290
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
When a scout comes home from camp

291
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
and he wakes up the next morning,

292
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,000
or maybe he wakes up the next afternoon

293
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and gets up and starts moving around the house

294
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
and doing what he does,

295
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
has the way that he looks at the world

296
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
and the way that he relates to other people changed?

297
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
well, if he's had a good experience at camp.

298
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
it certainly has,

299
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,000
And that is what I would approach parents with.

300
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
So, Dave, I hope that helps

301
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and I hope that begins to answer the question about that.

302
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
I would certainly like to hear your reflections

303
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
and your ideas about how you would promote

304
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:50,000
summer camp to parents who may be a little bit reluctant

305
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
to have to cut that check right now,

306
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,000
but you can get in touch with me

307
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
and you'll learn how to do that

308
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
at the end of the conversation.

309
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
This is Cliff Jacobson and you're listening to.

